WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.800
It seems like a lot of people have a particular&nbsp;
experience while getting into the EDH format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.400
and that experience goes something like this:&nbsp;
they find a commander and deck that they really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.880
gel with, like it speaks to them on some deep&nbsp;
level, and then they embark upon on a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.480
optimization quest, pouring increasing amounts of&nbsp;
time and money into making the deck as good as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:26.360
possibly can be. I went through this process about&nbsp;
ten years ago with my first commander deck, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.640
understand this impulse—you’ve got your baby, and&nbsp;
you want it to fly. This process can work to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.160
extent, especially if you have big piles of money&nbsp;
to throw at upgrading a deck, and I encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.520
everybody to put effort into making their decks&nbsp;
as enjoyable as possible for themselves, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.640
may mean power upgrades. That said, there are&nbsp;
actually some pretty compelling reasons *not* to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.000
upgrade a deck further once it plays smoothly at&nbsp;
a healthy middling power level. So in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.000
I’m going to go through these reasons, using one&nbsp;
of my mid-power decks to exemplify each of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.920
and then I’ll be talking about what decks&nbsp;
are the best candidates for an upgrade.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.560
The first reason not to upgrade a deck is&nbsp;
that some decks have natural bottlenecks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.160
in their functionality that makes it difficult&nbsp;
to give them a cohesive upgrade, and the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.400
common bottlenecks here are being vulnerable to&nbsp;
a particular type of hate or having a game plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.080
that is fundamentally difficult to speed up. If&nbsp;
your deck relies heavily on the board presence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.880
a commander that’s more than three mana, your&nbsp;
opponents only need to hate out your command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.480
zone a couple times early in order to shut you&nbsp;
down. If your deck heavily relies on building up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.760
a graveyard over the course of several turns, a&nbsp;
single piece of graveyard hate can totally blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.840
you out, and card quality improvements alone&nbsp;
won’t generally change this fact. If your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.240
heavily uses board wipes to maintain control over&nbsp;
the game, or something like goad for that matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.720
you’ll struggle against decks that don’t rely&nbsp;
on combat damage or which are more explosive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.120
which get more common at higher powers.&nbsp;
Ultimately, trying to improve a deck with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.360
a bottleneck like this will generally lead to&nbsp;
some very good games with the deck, but much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.320
worse consistency, with the deck getting dragged&nbsp;
down to the dirt by its bottlenecking factors a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.560
decent portion of the time. This is the reason I&nbsp;
don’t upgrade my Sharuum Slide deck—there are ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.960
to make it much more efficient at generating&nbsp;
value and controlling the game, but the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.880
has several vulnerabilities—its Astral Slides&nbsp;
getting hated out, its artifacts getting wiped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.000
and/or its graveyard getting exiled. The deck can&nbsp;
play through these things happening a little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.360
but decks generally run more of these things at&nbsp;
higher powers, and if it encounters too much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.000
these sorts of hate the deck will fall flat on&nbsp;
its face regardless of card quality upgrades.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.480
An extension on this problem is what I’ll call&nbsp;
“the cheap deck”. This is a deck that works pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.440
well at low budgets, but is very logarithmic in&nbsp;
terms of how it translates additional budget to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:47.080
additional power—it gets rapidly more expensive&nbsp;
to improve past a certain power level. The best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.960
example of this I can think of is Feather—it’s&nbsp;
a commander that has a decently beefy voltron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.880
gameplan if you build your deck specifically&nbsp;
around it, which usually means running a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.280
of 1-2 mana instants and sorceries that target&nbsp;
creatures along with some cards that work well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.960
with these sorts of spells. It’s hard to end&nbsp;
up with a Feather deck that’s bad, but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.240
also hard to build a Feather deck that’s really&nbsp;
good—1 mana instants and sorceries that target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.880
creatures are cheap so you’re already running&nbsp;
the best ones, meaning that the only option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.640
for trying to improve the deck is cutting some&nbsp;
of these for generic value cards in the colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.680
and the weakening of the Feather game plan that&nbsp;
this results in will negate some of that increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:29.080
in power. This reason is also why I don’t try to&nbsp;
improve my Meria deck: 1-2 mana equipments are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.600
the deck’s bread and butter, and there just aren’t&nbsp;
that many options in this category of card outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.840
of the dirt cheap ones I’m already running&nbsp;
and ones I consider prohibitively expensive.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.120
The other reason not to increase the power level&nbsp;
of a deck is if you simply don’t enjoy the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.880
level you end up at. If you’re playing in a group&nbsp;
of beatdown decks and everybody is relentlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.760
tuning the effectiveness of their beatdown, that&nbsp;
might feel okay in that context, but your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.880
might end up on the power level of a deck that&nbsp;
consistently combos off on turn 5 or voltron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:02.440
kills you on turn 4 or forcefully suppresses&nbsp;
your deck into the dirt with an unending stream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.520
of hate. And even if you’re still pulling off your&nbsp;
fair share of wins against these sorts of decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.120
as a player you need to ask yourself the question&nbsp;
of if that’s the sort of MTG you want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.200
playing. You also might need to slip combos&nbsp;
into your deck instead of winning in ways that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.360
play more to the deck’s strengths, which a lot of&nbsp;
players might not find to be a satisfying way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:25.600
go with their deck. This reason is why I leave my&nbsp;
Glissa deck at a middling power level—it’s a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.720
that wants to build up resources and then play&nbsp;
a bunch of big stuff, winning through gaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.520
board superiority and beating down its opponents&nbsp;
rather than playing some sort of explosive card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.600
like Torment of Hailfire or tutoring up a combo.&nbsp;
I play the deck to do beefy battlecruiser things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.720
and become the big bad of the table, not to pull&nbsp;
off a sneaky win when my opponents aren’t looking.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.600
Okay, so the point I’m trying to make here is&nbsp;
that some decks are less ideal candidates for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.880
upgrading past a certain point, you know,&nbsp;
you should maybe call them good enough. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.960
I do fully believe in this idea, but I think it’s&nbsp;
also useful to consider the terms here—what do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.240
mean by “upgrading” a deck? I’d say there are two&nbsp;
main factors that determine a deck’s power level:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.880
card quality—how good the cards are,&nbsp;
and cohesion—how well the cards are put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.840
together. I’d say that we as magic players tend&nbsp;
to overwhelmingly focus on the first one when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.520
think about a deck’s power level, particularly&nbsp;
when it comes to the question of upgrading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.000
and I actually think this makes perfect sense to&nbsp;
do. When you’re asking whether or not you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.640
improve your deck’s card quality, you’re talking&nbsp;
about an unambiguous exchange of resources—money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:34.560
for power level—whereas cohesion is a much more&nbsp;
challenging factor to modulate, particularly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.520
a deck that’s already fully-built. With that in&nbsp;
mind, I’d like to offer an additional framework:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.400
you can think of upgrading your deck’s&nbsp;
card quality as a tool for obtaining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.960
deck’s desired power level. If you have a&nbsp;
fundamentally janky premise for a deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.040
you want to end up decently powerful, running&nbsp;
good card quality can accomplish that goal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.200
some extent as long as you tread carefully&nbsp;
around potential bottlenecks. If you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.400
fundamentally powerful deck concept and you want&nbsp;
to play in a group of more middling power decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.480
you can restrict your card choice by&nbsp;
budget or theme or some other factor.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:10.840
But to this you might ask, what does it mean for&nbsp;
a deck concept to be fundamentally powerful or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:16.720
fundamentally weak? This takes me back to the core&nbsp;
question of this video—the question of when a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.560
should be upgraded with better card quality. I&nbsp;
mentioned Feather as an example of a deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:26.560
generally performs solidly on a low budget, but&nbsp;
which can be difficult and cost-inefficient to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.560
improve from that point. As a point of contrast,&nbsp;
I want to talk about a different popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.800
commander that, like Feather, tends to convert&nbsp;
a specific card type into resources for a fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.600
gameplan—Yuriko, the Tiger’s Shadow. Yuriko decks&nbsp;
span a range of power levels and contain a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:48.920
of variety in subthemes and card choices, but all&nbsp;
of them share a fundamental gameplan—play evasive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:53.840
creatures and/or Ninjas, and utilize these cards&nbsp;
with Yuriko to whittle down your opponents through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.200
a mixture of combat damage and Yuriko triggers,&nbsp;
generating card advantage in the process. Yuriko&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:03.920
notably doesn’t have the same ceiling as&nbsp;
Feather because she’s fundamentally a faster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:10.440
more resilient, and just simpler card. Commander&nbsp;
ninjutsu at two mana immediately puts her head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:15.440
and shoulders above Feather in terms of raw power,&nbsp;
and instead of being fiddly and requiring constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:21.040
upkeep or extra steps to reach her full power,&nbsp;
she converts low cost ninjas and evasive creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:27.040
into very fundamental resources of the game—card&nbsp;
advantage, and life loss for opponents—and can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:32.520
so with minimal mana investment. Decks can vary in&nbsp;
how many ninjas they run and what sorts of evasive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:37.560
creatures they play, but these cards are basically&nbsp;
guaranteed to be effective due to the speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:43.200
resilience, and simplicity of Yuriko, and as long&nbsp;
as a deck is above a certain minimum number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.520
these supporting creatures, these cards combine&nbsp;
with Yuriko to form a very powerful core to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:54.360
the deck. It’s this strong core for generating&nbsp;
generic resources that makes a deck especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:59.240
upgradable—there’s a good amount of diversity&nbsp;
in what a successful Yuriko deck can look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.720
and there are lots of piecemeal card quality&nbsp;
upgrades and packages that can be added to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.929
modulate the deck to whatever power level the&nbsp;
Yuriko player wants to be playing at. By contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.929 --> 00:08:08.021
core of a Feather deck is worse and less flexible&nbsp;
overall, which leaves flickering Dockside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.021 --> 00:08:08.120
Extortionist as one of the few genuinely strong&nbsp;
things the deck can do in high power casual games.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.600
I think it’s worth clarifying here that there’s&nbsp;
nothing wrong with commanders that are better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.560
suited to mid-power, nor is a commander like&nbsp;
Yuriko or Korvold that inherently pushes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:22.280
higher power necessarily more interesting or more&nbsp;
worthwhile. There are also the players who like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.520
the challenge of trying to boost a commander&nbsp;
or theme that isn’t inherently high power up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.680
to that point, and that can be a fun process.&nbsp;
I personally like the challenge of building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.360
powerful decks on a low budget, as it forces&nbsp;
you to put a lot of effort into the cohesion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.600
of the deck since the card quality is going to be&nbsp;
naturally a bit lacking. At the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.240
I just want people to know what they’re getting&nbsp;
into when they decide to pour time and money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:49.920
into a deck. If you have a limit on how much&nbsp;
you want to spend on decks, it’s valuable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:53.680
know when you’re dealing with a commander who&nbsp;
will naturally take more money to boost from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:59.600
mid-power deck to a high-power deck, and as stated&nbsp;
earlier, this will fundamentally come down to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.640
powerful a deck’s core resource-generating&nbsp;
and game-winning pieces are. And even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.200
budget isn’t a concern, it’s worth keeping an&nbsp;
eye on a deck’s weak points and making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.680
that they aren’t going to be exacerbated&nbsp;
by playing against more powerful decks.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.640
I feel like there’s a culture among a lot of EDH&nbsp;
players of higher power decks being considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.280
cooler or more interesting, which leads to this&nbsp;
sort of rat race of chasing generic staples and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.720
applying formulaic rules to every deck to bump&nbsp;
up its power level. And I don’t want to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.000
anybody not to improve a deck they want to&nbsp;
improve, but I just want to tell you right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.840
now that if you have a mid-power deck you&nbsp;
really like, you don’t necessarily need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:41.120
change it. If you do want a higher power deck,&nbsp;
you can pick out a couple things you like from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:46.400
an existing deck you enjoy such as a card or a&nbsp;
theme, and then build a new, more powerful home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:55.320
for it. This also goes for putting pet cards&nbsp;
in decks, but that’s a topic for another day.

